<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Douro</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Duero, Douro River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Douro is one of the major rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, flowing about 897 km from the Sierra de Urbión in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> westwards across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal to the Atlantic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>. In antiquity its basin was home to peoples such as the Vaccaei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a>, Vettones and Callaeci, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it marked the frontier zone between the Christian north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Its Portuguese valley is famous for port wine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/douro.jpg" alt="Douro" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitor Oliveira from Torres Vedras, PORTUGAL · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_Douro_-_Portugal_(32615481975)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
